# 5. NON-NEGOTIABLE ARCHITECTURAL RULES

## Rule 1 — Deterministic dispatch

Emergency-to-resource mapping belongs entirely to Python.

Implement it in:

orchestrator/dispatch.py

The reasoning model classifies the emergency and extracts facts.

Python determines required resource categories.

Python checks synthetic resource availability.

Python allocates simulated assignments.

The LLM must never control the dispatch decision.

## Rule 2 — No model-generated operational data

Only backend code may generate:
- Resource IDs.
- Assignment IDs.
- Dispatch references.
- Simulated availability.
- Simulated ETAs.
- Assignment statuses.
- Incident IDs.

## Rule 3 — Absolute fact provenance

Every LLM-extracted incident fact MUST reference its source transcript segment.

Use:

source_utterance_id

or an equivalent validated reference to the exact transcript segment.

Where available, preserve the supporting text span.

If a value is missing:
- Keep it null or unknown.
- Never guess.
- Never fill it from assumptions.

If statements conflict:
- Preserve both statements.
- Record their source utterances.
- Flag the conflict.

This requirement applies to every LLM-extracted incident fact.

### Backend-generated facts have separate provenance

Not every application fact originates in speech.

GPS coordinates, hospital records, landmark records, synthetic resource records and tool results must use their own typed provenance.

Use an application-owned provenance structure with fields such as:

- source_type
- source_id
- source_timestamp
- source_metadata

Supported conceptual source types include:
- caller_utterance
- browser_gps
- database_record
- provider_response
- backend_rule
- human_review

Only caller-derived extracted facts require source_utterance_id.

Examples:
- A caller saying a person is trapped requires source_utterance_id.
- Browser GPS coordinates require browser_gps provenance.
- A hospital capability requires database_record provenance.
- A synthetic resource assignment requires backend_rule and database provenance.
- A classification result must reference the caller utterances supporting it.

Never attach fabricated utterance IDs to backend-generated facts.

## Rule 4 — No free-form medical or first-aid advice

STAY WITH ME is limited to:
- Safety guidance.
- Emotional reassurance.
- Situation explanation.
- Relevant follow-up questions.
- Honest status communication.

Do not generate medical diagnoses, treatment decisions or first-aid procedures.

Use an approved, pre-reviewed instruction library.

The reasoning model may select an eligible instruction but cannot invent or rewrite safety protocols.

Every instruction must include:
- Instruction ID.
- Emergency category.
- Approved text.
- Language.
- Source.
- Review date.
- Version.

If no applicable reviewed instruction exists, provide calm general reassurance and request appropriate human assistance.

## Rule 5 — Silence does not mean unconsciousness

Never infer unconsciousness, death, injury or abandonment from silence alone.

Follow the timeout policy.

## Rule 6 — No speculative tool execution

Never execute operational tools while the caller's utterance is incomplete.

Never send partial transcripts to the reasoning model. Reasoning requests use finalized utterances only.

Wait for utterance finalization.

Validate extracted facts.

Check permissions and session state.

Then execute the approved backend operation.

## Rule 7 — No Java or Spring Boot

Use Python and FastAPI exclusively for the backend.

No Java services.

No Spring Boot.

## Rule 8 — No Monte Carlo

Do not add Monte Carlo simulations or unrelated probabilistic systems.

Hospital ranking and dispatch use deterministic rules.

## Rule 9 — No invented provider contracts

When documentation is incomplete, stop and ask.

Do not pretend an application abstraction is the provider's native API contract.

