# 14. DUPLICATE-INCIDENT DETECTION

Implement in:

orchestrator/duplicates.py

Flag a new incident as a possible duplicate when all conditions hold:

1. Same normalised emergency type.
2. Confirmed incident coordinates within 200 metres.
3. Incident creation timestamps within 5 minutes.

"Normalised" means mapped to the fixed taxonomy in Section 9. Incidents whose category is `unknown` are not compared for duplicate detection until they are reclassified to a supported category.

Use PostGIS for distance calculations.

Use suitable indexes for incident time and location queries.

### When detection runs

An incident does not yet have a category or a confirmed location when it is created. Duplicate detection therefore runs synchronously at the first moment BOTH of the following are true for the incident:
- It has a supported category at or above the confidence threshold.
- It has a confirmed location (Section 12.3).

It must re-run whenever the category or confirmed location changes. Each run is idempotent: it must not create duplicate flags or duplicate audit events for the same incident pair and rule. The query is bounded to the 5-minute window and the 200-metre radius to keep it fast.

Do not use uncertain coordinates as confirmed duplicate evidence.

When a match is found:

- Set possible_duplicate = true.
- Record matched incident ID.
- Record distance.
- Record time difference.
- Record the match rule.
- Set control_room_review_required = true.
- Create an audit event.

Never automatically merge incidents.

Do not delete the new incident.

Do not automatically cancel assignments.

Do not assume nearby emergencies are the same event.

The simulated reviewer must be able to inspect both incidents and decide whether to associate them.

