# 21. DATABASE DESIGN AND AUDIT

Use PostgreSQL 16 with PostGIS and pg_trgm.

The database must support:

- Sessions.
- Session state transitions.
- Session event sequences.
- Incidents.
- Transcript segments.
- Extracted facts and provenance.
- Incident review flags.
- Possible duplicate references.
- Landmarks, with source and synthetic-or-approved labelling.
- Hospital directory records.
- Hospital score breakdowns.
- Synthetic resources.
- Synthetic resource assignments.
- Approved instruction versions.
- Audio asset manifests.
- Append-only audit events.

Use foreign keys, constraints and indexes.

Use migrations.

Audit events reference sessions, incidents and other entities by pseudonymous ID without cascading foreign keys to deletable personal-data tables, so retention deletion never requires mutating or blocking on audit records.

## 21.1 Session persistence

PostgreSQL is the authoritative source of truth.

Persist important state transitions before publishing the result to the frontend.

Use transactions where a state transition and associated event must commit together.

Support WebSocket reconnection using session identity and the last event sequence.

Prevent duplicate event processing.

## 21.2 Append-only audit enforcement

Append-only must be enforced at the database level.

Do not rely only on application conventions.

Use separate database roles.

Migration/owner role:
- Owns schema objects.
- Creates tables, triggers and migrations.

Runtime application role:
- Can SELECT and INSERT audit events.
- Cannot UPDATE audit events.
- Cannot DELETE audit events.
- Cannot TRUNCATE audit events.
- Does not own the audit table.

Apply explicit PostgreSQL permission restrictions.

Add database triggers that reject:
- UPDATE.
- DELETE.
- TRUNCATE.

The application runtime role must not be able to disable or remove these triggers.

If an audit record needs correction, append a new corrective event.

Never modify historical audit entries.

## 21.3 Audit information

Record timestamps for:
- Session creation.
- Final transcript events.
- Reasoning-model analysis.
- Provider-blocked turns and outage events.
- Fact validation.
- Backend tool requests.
- Location queries.
- Hospital queries.
- Resource lookups.
- Simulated assignment creation.
- Holding phrase playback.
- TTS first byte.
- Barge-in (including false barge-in).
- TTS cancellation.
- Timeouts.
- Duplicate detection.
- Reclassification.
- Incident flag changes.
- Session completion.

Record:
- Session correlation ID.
- Incident ID.
- Event type.
- Severity (info, warning or critical).
- Timestamp.
- Outcome.
- Error code and reason code where applicable.
- Provenance references.

Redact PII before writing persistent audit records.

## 21.4 Retention and deletion

Persistent transcript and other personal data must have a documented, configurable retention policy.

They must be deletable:
- On session closure where the configured policy requires it.
- When their configured retention period expires.
- Through an authorised deletion workflow.

Separate transcript content and personal data from immutable audit event metadata.

The audit log must not contain unnecessary raw audio or unrestricted transcript content.

Audit records should retain pseudonymous IDs and event metadata rather than duplicate personal data.

Deleting retained transcript content must not require mutating historical append-only audit records.

Document the retention configuration and deletion behaviour.

