# 26. EVALUATION DATASET AND TEST MATRIX

Evaluation must be systematic.

Do not report model accuracy or latency based on a handful of demonstrations.

Build a synthetic evaluation set with two parts.

## 26.0 Structure

Part A — Single-utterance matrix (scenarios 1–10 below):

10 scenarios × 4 language conditions = 40 utterances per speaker.

The four language conditions are:

1. Telugu.
2. Hindi.
3. English.
4. Code-mixed speech.

The code-mixed condition contains two sub-conditions: Telugu-English (Tanglish) and Hindi-English (Hinglish). Report sample counts for each sub-condition separately, and report the combined code-mixed figure only alongside them.

Use short 2–3 second utterances for dedicated short-clip STT tests.

Part B — Multi-turn scripted conversations (scenarios 11–12 below):

These require context across turns and are not multiplied by language conditions. Script each as one or more multi-turn conversations (for example scenario 11 starts in one language and switches mid-session). Report them separately from the Part A matrix.

Suggested scenario coverage:

1. Medical emergency.
2. Fire.
3. Fire with reported injuries.
4. Road accident.
5. Building collapse and trapped persons.
6. Physical threat or attack.
7. Gas leak.
8. Explosion.
9. Unknown or ambiguous emergency.
10. Poor GPS and spoken landmark resolution.
11. Mid-session language switching (Part B).
12. Possible duplicate or possible false/prank review (Part B).

Include violence and injury descriptions (scenarios 4, 6 and 8) specifically so provider safety-filter behaviour is measured (Section 26.5).

Use synthetic scripts and consented speakers.

Never use real emergency recordings without authorisation.

## 26.1 Minimum viable evaluation

If the full matrix cannot be completed within available time, the following is the smallest publishable evaluation:

- 6 scenarios × 3 language conditions × 2 speakers = 36 unique utterances.
- The three language conditions are fixed as Telugu, Hindi and code-mixed. English is the condition dropped in the minimum viable case. Code-mixed MUST NOT be dropped.
- The code-mixed condition (6 scenarios × 2 speakers = 12 utterances) is split evenly between Tanglish and Hinglish (6 utterances each), with each sub-condition reported separately.
- Each utterance recorded for latency in 2 runs, giving 72 total measurement observations.

This set supports overall percentile reporting only when at least 30 valid observations exist overall. It does not support per-language-condition percentiles: it yields 24 observations per language condition, which is below the 30-observation threshold in Section 26.4. Report per-language counts and mark per-language percentiles as insufficiently sampled.

Anything smaller than this must be labelled "exploratory" and no percentile claims should be made.

Do not silently shrink the evaluation set. Report exactly what was completed and what was not.

## 26.2 Speaker diversity

For each language condition, collect recordings from at least three distinct consented speakers where the full matrix is completed. In the minimum viable case, two distinct speakers per language condition.

Where the English condition is included (full matrix), include at least two different accents.

Do not base the entire evaluation on one speaker.

Record anonymised speaker IDs for evaluation reproducibility.

Avoid storing unnecessary personal information about participants.

## 26.3 Ground truth

For every utterance, maintain human-reviewed ground truth for:
- Transcript.
- Language.
- Emergency category.
- Extracted facts.
- Expected resource categories.
- Location information where present.
- Missing information.
- Expected fallback behaviour.

Do not use model-generated transcripts as their own ground truth.

Document code-mixed spelling and transcription normalisation.

## 26.4 Repeated latency tests

For latency measurements:
- Run each evaluation utterance three times where practical (two runs in the minimum viable evaluation).
- Record successful and failed requests.
- Exclude warm-up runs from reported percentiles.
- Keep environment and provider configuration fixed.
- Report exact sample counts.

For each language condition, report p50 and p95 only when at least 30 valid observations exist.

Otherwise report the sample count and mark the percentile as insufficiently sampled.

Do not imply a small evaluation set establishes statistically robust production performance.

## 26.5 Metrics

Speech recognition:
- WER.
- CER.
- Per-language transcript accuracy.
- Code-mixed transcript quality.

Classification:
- Accuracy.
- Macro F1.
- Per-category precision.
- Per-category recall.
- Unknown fallback correctness.
- Provider-block rate per scenario and language condition, reported separately from classification accuracy.
- Provider-block fallback correctness (the fallback fires at exactly N consecutive blocks and never earlier).

Entity extraction:
- Field-level precision.
- Field-level recall.
- Field-level F1.
- Provenance coverage.

Location:
- GPS threshold handling accuracy.
- Landmark candidate accuracy.
- Confirmation rate.
- Unresolved-location rate.

Dispatch:
